    "q-337102": {
      "dom_id": "step-q337102",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2{e}^{3t}$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{10}{3}$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td></td>\n\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}{e}^{t}-0\\cdot {e}^{t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -2{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 4{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2}{3}{e}^{3t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2}{3}{e}^{3t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
